@* The phone's home screen — a separate form from the desktop Dashboard, not a restyling of it.

   The desktop dashboard opens with a 200px saffron hero and lays its sadhana summary out as a
   wrapping flex row that collapses to three stacked rows on a 375px screen. That is a lot of
   scrolling before a seeker reaches anything they can act on, so this page is built the other way
   round: the greeting is one line, the sadhana summary is one row, then what is special today —
   the Panchangam, today's bhakti — then what is due now (prayers), the browse strip (chants),
   and last the quick-action launcher, which the bottom bar and the More sheet already cover.

   Every destination is filtered through MenuState, the same Modules tree the bottom bar is built
   from, so the home screen never offers a seeker a door that opens onto access-denied.

   Every top-level block here is a DIRECT child of .scm-content on purpose. That container supplies
   the 12px rhythm via `gap` and the page gutter via `padding`, and mobile.css targets
   `.scm-content > *`. Wrapping this page in a single root div would silently lose both. *@
@page "/mobile-dashboard"
@attribute [Authorize]
@inherits LocalizedComponentBase
@implements IDisposable
@inject IApiClient Api
@inject MenuState Menus

@if (_loading)
{
    <OmLoader FullPage="true" Message='@Loc["dashboard.loading", "Loading your sadhana…"]' />
}
else if (_model is not null)
{
    @* One line, not a hero. The logo tile is 40px rather than the desktop's 82px, and the seeker's
       name carries the identity that the desktop spends a whole <h1> on. *@
    <section class="scm-greet">
        <span class="scm-greet-om"><AppLogo Bare="true" /></span>
        <span class="scm-greet-text">
            <span class="scm-greet-kicker">@Greeting</span>
            <span class="scm-greet-name">@DisplayName</span>
        </span>
    </section>

    @if (_streak is not null)
    {
        <div class="sc-section-head">
            <h2>🙏 @Loc["sadhana.mySadhana", "My Sadhana"]</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="sc-card scm-sadhana">
            @* Four cells, one row, always. The streak is the lead cell and reads larger; the other
               three are equal. A CSS grid rather than a flex row so the cells cannot wrap onto a
               second line no matter how many digits a long-practising seeker accumulates. *@
            <div class="scm-sadhana-row">
                <div class="scm-sadhana-cell scm-sadhana-streak @(_streak.CurrentStreak > 0 ? "" : "cold")">
                    <span class="scm-sadhana-n">
                        <span class="scm-sadhana-flame" aria-hidden="true">🔥</span>@_streak.CurrentStreak
                    </span>
                    <span class="scm-sadhana-l">@Loc["sadhana.dayStreak", "Day Streak"]</span>
                </div>
                <div class="scm-sadhana-cell">
                    <span class="scm-sadhana-n">@_streak.TotalMalas</span>
                    <span class="scm-sadhana-l">@Loc["sadhana.totalMalas", "Total Malas"]</span>
                </div>
                <div class="scm-sadhana-cell">
                    <span class="scm-sadhana-n">@_streak.LongestStreak</span>
                    <span class="scm-sadhana-l">@Loc["sadhana.longest", "Longest"]</span>
                </div>
                <div class="scm-sadhana-cell">
                    <span class="scm-sadhana-n">@_streak.TotalDaysPracticed</span>
                    <span class="scm-sadhana-l">@Loc["common.days", "Days"]</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="scm-sadhana-foot">
                <span class="sc-streak-today @(_streak.PracticedToday ? "done" : "pending")">
                    @(_streak.PracticedToday
                        ? "✓ " + Loc["sadhana.practicedToday", "Practiced Today"]
                        : Loc["sadhana.notYetToday", "Not Yet Today"])
                </span>
                @* Gated on the menu, not on the streak having loaded. The API grant and the phone
                   menu are separate switches (a form also needs Show in mobile to reach the
                   phone), and the link has to follow the one that decides whether the page opens. *@
                @if (Menus.CanOpen("sadhana"))
                {
                    <a href="sadhana" class="scm-sadhana-cta">@Loc["sadhana.continueSadhana", "Continue Sadhana"] →</a>
                }
            </div>
        </div>
    }

    @* Straight after the seeker's own summary: the one block that is about the day itself. The
       summary is useful on its own, so it stays for a seeker without the Panchangam form; only
       the link to the full page goes. *@
    <PanchangamToday Linked='@Menus.CanOpen("panchangam")' />

    @* Today's deity and its sadhana, after the day's Panchangam. Kept because it is the one block
       that changes daily and is the reason to open the app at all. The hardcoded five-deity "Daily Darshan" strip from the
       desktop dashboard is deliberately NOT repeated here — this component shows the same subject
       with real data, and two deity carousels on one phone screen is one too many. *@
    <TodayBhakti />

    @* The time-of-day prayers: the one block with a deadline. Three rows, the current one first, and the rest behind "Show all" — the full list
       is a day's worth, most of it already past or hours away. Gated on Sadhana because every
       prayer opens sadhana/chant/{id}; without that form each row would be a dead end. *@
    @if (Menus.CanOpen("sadhana"))
    {
        <PrayerTimes MaxItems="3" />
    }

    @* After the day's blocks, because it is for browsing rather than for today. Same Sadhana gate
       as the prayers: every chant in a category opens sadhana/chant/{id}. *@
    @if (Menus.CanOpen("sadhana"))
    {
        <ChantCategories />
    }
    @* Last on the page: the bottom bar and the More sheet are the launchers a thumb reaches for,
       so these tiles are a convenience after the day's content rather than the reason to open it.

       Reuses the More sheet's tile system (.scm-quickgrid / .scm-quick / .scm-quick-ic /
       .scm-quick-label) rather than inventing a second one, so the two launchers in this app stay
       one design. .scm-home-quick only pins the track count — the sheet's auto-fill would give 3
       or 4 columns depending on width, and the home screen wants exactly 4, so eight tiles are two
       even rows.

       Each tile is shown only when the seeker's menu holds its route — the same check the bottom
       bar and the More sheet make, so a tile never leads to access-denied. Profile is the one
       exception: /profile has no menu row and its endpoints are exempt, so everyone can open it.
       The section waits for the menu rather than guessing: rendering all eight and then removing
       some would reflow the two rows under the seeker's thumb.

       /chants is deliberately NOT a tile. That route is the admin Chants master, gated on the
       "chants" module, which a seeker is never granted. Seekers reach chants through Sadhana and
       the Chants strip at the foot of this page. *@
    var tiles = Menus.Menu is null ? new List<Tile>() : Tiles.Where(t => t.AlwaysOn || Menus.CanOpen(t.Route)).ToList();
    @if (tiles.Count > 0)
    {
        <div class="sc-section-head">
            <h2>⚡ @Loc["mobileDashboard.quickActions", "Quick Actions"]</h2>
        </div>
        <nav class="scm-quickgrid scm-home-quick">
            @foreach (var t in tiles)
            {
                <a class="scm-quick" href="@t.Route">
                    <span class="scm-quick-ic" aria-hidden="true">@t.Icon</span>
                    <span class="scm-quick-label">@t.Label(Loc)</span>
                </a>
            }
        </nav>
    }

}
else
{
    <div class="sc-alert sc-alert-error">@_error</div>
}

@code {
    private DashboardModel? _model;
    private SadhanaStreak? _streak;
    private bool _loading = true;
    private string? _error;

    /// <summary>One quick-action tile. Route is both the href and the menu route it is gated on.</summary>
    private sealed record Tile(string Route, string Icon, Func<LocalizationState, string> Label, bool AlwaysOn = false);

    // Each label is a literal Loc["key", "fallback"] call rather than a Key/Fallback pair of fields:
    // LocalizationKeyUsageTests finds keys by matching `Loc["…` in the .razor source, so a key held
    // in a string field would be invisible to it and a renamed key would fall back to English
    // forever without failing the build. The lambda parameter is named Loc for exactly that match.
    private static readonly Tile[] Tiles =
    {
        new("sadhana", "📿", Loc => Loc["mobileDashboard.sadhana", "Sadhana"]),
        new("panchangam", "📅", Loc => Loc["mobileDashboard.panchangam", "Panchangam"]),
        new("puja-process", "🪔", Loc => Loc["pujaProcess.title", "Puja Process"]),
        new("favorites", "⭐", Loc => Loc["mobileDashboard.favorites", "Favorites"]),
        new("wallpapers-download", "🖼️", Loc => Loc["mobileDashboard.wallpapers", "Wallpapers"]),
        new("my-notifications", "🔔", Loc => Loc["mobileDashboard.reminders", "Reminders"]),
        new("feedback", "💬", Loc => Loc["mobileDashboard.feedback", "Feedback"]),
        new("profile", "👤", Loc => Loc["mobileDashboard.profile", "Profile"], AlwaysOn: true),
    };

    private string DisplayName => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_model?.SeekerName)
        ? _model?.FullName ?? string.Empty
        : _model!.SeekerName!;

    private string Greeting
    {
        get
        {
            var hour = DateTime.Now.Hour;
            return hour < 12 ? Loc["auth.greetingMorning", "Shubh Prabhat · Good Morning"]
                 : hour < 17 ? Loc["auth.greetingAfternoon", "Shubh Dopahar · Good Afternoon"]
                 : Loc["auth.greetingEvening", "Shubh Sandhya · Good Evening"];
        }
    }

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        await base.OnInitializedAsync();

        // Subscribed before the load so a reload (MenuRefreshService) or a language switch that
        // lands while the dashboard request is in flight still re-renders the gated blocks. On the
        // phone the layout has already started this load, so the call shares its task; opened
        // under the desktop layout, this is the only thing that loads the menu at all.
        //
        // Started before the dashboard request and awaited after it, so the two round trips overlap
        // instead of queueing — but still awaited before the loader goes, so the gated tiles are
        // in place on the first paint rather than popping in and reflowing the page. EnsureLoadedAsync
        // never throws (a failed fetch is an empty menu), so the finally still clears the loader.
        Menus.OnChanged += MenuChanged;
        var menu = Menus.EnsureLoadedAsync();

        try
        {
            _model = await Api.GetDashboardAsync();
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            _error = Loc["dashboard.couldNotLoad", "Could not load the dashboard."] + " " + ex.Message;
        }
        finally
        {
            await menu;
            _loading = false;
        }

        // Best-effort, exactly as on the desktop dashboard. The streak endpoint names this form as
        // well as Sadhana, so a seeker granted only the mobile home still gets the card — which is
        // why its "Continue Sadhana" link is gated on the menu separately above. Do not rely on
        // this catch to hide anything on a denial: a module 403 is turned into a navigation to
        // access-denied by ForbiddenHandler before the exception ever reaches here.
        try { _streak = await Api.GetSadhanaStreakAsync(); }
        catch { /* leave the block hidden */ }
    }

    private void MenuChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    public override void Dispose()
    {
        Menus.OnChanged -= MenuChanged;
        base.Dispose();
    }
}
